00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:19.340
The concept is a research lab comes across
a technology that would disable all firearms

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:22.810
when a device is placed in proximity.

00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:27.750
It's almost like a mobile phone jammer except
it's a firearm jammer.

00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:32.260
And at first it seems like this is going to
be a fantastic thing.

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:36.170
This is fiction by the way, just restating
that this is going to be a fantastic thing

00:03:36.170 --> 00:03:38.400
for stopping gun violence.

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:45.480
It could be used by militaries to, uh, fight
wars without weapons being used.

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:51.800
The idea of it sounds fantastic, but then
Arthur C. Clark, as, as he is known to do,

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:56.590
gives us the other side of seemingly utopian,
uh, circumstances.

00:03:56.590 --> 00:04:00.610
And there's not any political statement implicit
in what I'm saying.

00:04:00.610 --> 00:04:02.280
This is just what the story is.

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:07.030
The trigger by Arthur C. Clark written along
with Michael Cube McDowell, excellent book,

00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:11.510
20 years old, extremely still relevant today.

